sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Decide the machine on eventually periodic tapes" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3> ;
    dct:description "Try to refute conventional universality by a regularity theorem. Fix an eventually periodic initial tape with left period q, right period p and a finite center of length L. The head will destroy periodicity, so the configuration space is not the finite set of phases. The attack is to find an invariant of machine 596440 that restores finiteness: a bound on the number of non-periodic cells, or a vector of local statistics (for example counts of 1s and 2s modulo a small integer) that determines the future and takes values in a finite monoid. If every space-time diagram from an eventually periodic tape is generated by a finite automaton whose state is that invariant, then halting and reachability of any finite target pattern are decidable, which refutes universality under the conventional restriction. The invariant has to be proved for all p, q and L, not checked up to a bound. A bound-only result belongs to the search approach and is not this theorem." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A no-sorry theorem that reachability for machine 596440 on every eventually periodic tape is decidable." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Every candidate finite invariant fails on an explicit tape: the non-periodic region grows without a periodic residue. That kills this proof route and leaves conventional universality open." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/proof" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the configuration graph of machine 596440 on eventually periodic tapes. 2. Search symbolically for a finite invariant by enumerating small moduli and local window statistics. 3. If a candidate invariant closes, prove it is preserved by all six transitions. 4. Prove that the invariant's state, plus the head state, determines the next window. 5. Conclude decidability of reachability, which is the anti-universality theorem." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): long-shot" ;
    skos:note "Missing: No known finite invariant for this machine. Finding one is open mathematics, not a missing dataset." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: turing-machine-enumeration" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-27> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-28> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-23> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.wolframscience.com/prizes/tm23/technicaldetails.html> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.29.1.1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2008.09.051> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/smallest-universal-turing-machine-2-3> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Settle whether the (2,3) Turing machine is universal under a conventional definition" .

  <https://www.wolframscience.com/prizes/tm23/technicaldetails.html> dct:title "Wolfram 2,3 Turing machine technical details" ;
    dct:description "The six transitions of machine 596440." .
  <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.29.1.1> dct:title "Universality of Wolfram’s 2, 3 Turing Machine" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Smith 2020, the non-periodic background this approach is not allowed to use" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2008.09.051> dct:title "The complexity of small universal Turing machines: A survey" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Woods and Neary 2009, the blank and periodic-background convention for small universal machines" .
}

